.TH SPEEDNS 1 "August 2026" "SpeeDNS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
speedns \- compare recursive DNS resolver performance
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B speedns
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B speedns run
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B speedns completion
.BR bash | zsh | fish | powershell
.br
.B speedns resolvers
.br
.B speedns corpus
.br
.B speedns version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B speedns
is a read-only command-line benchmark for recursive DNS resolvers. It measures
UDP, TCP, DNS-over-HTTPS, DNS-over-TLS, and dedicated DNS-over-QUIC separately.
It does not change system DNS settings, require root privileges, or send
telemetry.
.PP
The default report compares the bundled resolver catalog using a reproducible
sample of domain names and shows a recommendation for each selected transport.
Use JSON or CSV output when another program needs the aggregate results.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B speedns
Run a benchmark. The command is also the default when no subcommand is given.
.TP
.B speedns run
Run a benchmark explicitly.
.TP
.B speedns completion SHELL
Write a completion script for SHELL, which may be bash, zsh, fish, or
PowerShell.
.TP
.B speedns resolvers
List bundled resolver ownership, policy, addresses, and supported transports.
.TP
.B speedns corpus
Show the embedded domain corpus provenance and checksum.
.TP
.B speedns version
Show version, commit, and build information.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --protocol udp,tcp,doh,dot,doq
Select the transports to benchmark. Defaults to udp,tcp,doh,dot,doq; a
transport no selected resolver declares is skipped rather than failed.
.TP
.B --resolver NAME=URI
Add a custom resolver endpoint. Repeat the option for multiple endpoints.
.TP
.B --resolver-file PATH
Load strict, versioned resolver profiles from YAML.
.TP
.B --no-defaults
Do not include the bundled public resolver catalog. Use with
.B --resolver
or
.B --resolver-file
to benchmark only your own endpoints.
.TP
.B --family 4|6|both|auto
Select the resolver address family. The default
.B auto
uses the local up-interface address inventory without performing DNS lookups or
connection probes.
.TP
.B --domains PATH
Read one domain per line from a custom list. Blank lines and comments are
ignored. Underscored service labels such as _dmarc.example.com are accepted;
the underscore is only allowed as the first character of a label.
.TP
.B --sample N
Sample N domains from the selected corpus. Defaults to 100.
.TP
.B --skip-invalid-domains
Skip entries in
.B --domains
that are not usable domain names instead of failing the run. The report warns
how many were dropped, and the recorded corpus size and checksum describe the
names actually measured. A list with no usable entry still fails.
.TP
.B --cache-miss
Opt in to bounded random names below the reserved example.com zone. It cannot
be combined with --domains or --full. Each resolver is measured exactly once
and each generated name is asked exactly one question, because a resolver holds
one cache across its transports and addresses and caches a negative answer for
the whole name. Profiles that share an address or a TLS name (a DoH URL host or
a server_name) count as one resolver, measured over the earliest declared
profile's first transport at its earliest declared address. Dropped endpoints
are named in a report warning. A cache-miss
run therefore cannot produce a sound transport comparison, and speedns diff
refuses cache-miss reports by design. The rules keep any name from being asked
twice at one resolver; they do not prove the resolver recursed, because a
validating resolver using RFC 8198 aggressive NSEC/NSEC3 synthesis can answer
an unseen name from a cached denial proof, including one cached for a name
SpeeDNS generated earlier in the run.
.TP
.B --cache-miss-sample N
Generate N unique reserved-zone names, at most 20 and 20 by default, for
--cache-miss. Each name is asked one question, so N is also the scored sample
count. The
generated names are still a corpus that --sample draws from: when --sample is
smaller than N, only that many names are measured and the report warns about
the truncation. Set --sample to N or higher, as the default of 100 is, to
measure every generated name.
.TP
.B --full
Use the complete selected domain list.
.TP
.B --seed N
Use a specific seed so the selected query order can be reproduced.
.TP
.B --type A,AAAA
Select DNS record types, defaulting to A,AAAA. Zone-transfer types
(AXFR, IXFR), meta-types (ANY, MAILA, MAILB, TSIG, TKEY) and the OPT
pseudo-record are rejected.
.TP
.B --timeout DURATION
Set the per-query and connection timeout, for example 2s. Every measured round
waits for its slowest dispatched target, so a target that never answers costs
the whole protocol group up to one timeout per measured query. Per protocol,
unresponsive targets add roughly queries per target \(mu
ceil(unresponsive / concurrency) \(mu timeout: one silent target in a default
run adds about 200 \(mu 2s. There is no cutoff; --family, a smaller resolver
selection, or a lower --timeout shortens such a run.
.TP
.B --concurrency N
Set the maximum number of targets prepared at once, of measured exchanges in
flight, and of targets receiving --dnssec probes, per transport. Defaults to 4.
Every measured round waits about ceil(unresponsive / N) timeouts for targets
that never answer, so each further group of up to N unresponsive targets adds
another timeout to every round.
.TP
.B --format table|json|csv
Choose human-readable, JSON, or CSV output. Defaults to table.
.TP
.B --output PATH
Write the report to a file instead of standard output. A regular file is
replaced atomically: the report is written next to the destination and renamed
over it only after a successful run, so a failed run leaves the previous file
untouched, and the replacement receives the same permissions an ordinary shell
redirection would create, narrowed by the current umask. Destinations that cannot be replaced that way are written in place
instead, which covers non-regular files such as /dev/null and named pipes,
plus a writable file in a directory that rejects new entries. On Unix systems
a regular file with more than one hard link is also written in place, so every
name shows the new report. When the final rename is refused because the
destination is a mount point, such as a single-file bind mount, the finished
report is copied into it rather than discarded; if that copy fails, the
temporary file holding the complete report is kept, readable only by its
owner, and named in the error.
Writing in place gives up atomicity: an in-place destination can keep partial
output after a failed run or write. An existing file the caller cannot write
is refused rather than replaced, as a shell redirection would refuse it. A
directory is rejected, and so is a socket named by its own path, because a
socket cannot be opened by name.
.IP
On Unix systems the descriptor aliases /dev/stdout, /dev/stderr, /dev/fd/N,
/proc/self/fd/N and /proc/thread-self/fd/N, spelled exactly like that, are not
opened at all. The report is written to the descriptor the alias names, 1 for
/dev/stdout, 2 for /dev/stderr and N for the others, the way a report without
.B --output
is written to standard output; this covers the /dev/fd/N paths of process
substitution. So
.B --output /dev/stdout >> run.log
appends to run.log and
.B > run.log
replaces its contents, just as omitting
.B --output
would, and a socket on standard output, as under systemd's
StandardOutput=journal, receives the report. The descriptor must be one
SpeeDNS inherited from the shell or whatever started it, must be open for
writing and must not be a directory, so
.B --output /dev/fd/0 < file
is refused. A number nobody handed to SpeeDNS counts as closed even when
SpeeDNS uses it internally: the Go runtime holds descriptors of its own,
such as 3 (and 4 on Linux), so
.B --output /dev/fd/3
without a 3> redirection fails before the first query instead of writing the
report into them. Descriptors 0, 1 and 2 are never found closed: the Go
runtime opens any of them that is closed at startup on /dev/null, so
.B --output /dev/stdout >&-
discards the report without an error, as a run without
.B --output
would. One difference from plain standard output remains. When the reader of
a pipe has already gone, as in
.BR "--output /dev/stdout | true" ,
writing the report fails with a broken-pipe error and status 2, while a run
without
.B --output
is ended by SIGPIPE. A symlink of the caller's own is written through only
when it points at a named pipe, a terminal or other character device. Any
other symlink destination, including one to a regular file, a directory or a
socket and a dangling one, is rejected before writing, leaving the link and
its referent unchanged.
.IP
The destination is checked before the first query is sent, so a missing
directory, a rejected symlink, an unwritable file or a closed or read-only
descriptor exits with status 2 without running the benchmark. It is checked
again when the report is written.
.TP
.B --details
Include cold latency, jitter, response outcomes, expanded diagnostics, and
every row the default table collapses.
.TP
.B --raw
Include per-query observations in JSON output.
.TP
.B --profile-view
Show same-resolver transport comparisons with score confidence intervals.
Requires table or JSON output.
.TP
.B --redact-system
Redact local system resolver addresses and labels in reports.
.TP
.B --assert EXPRESSION
Assert a winner metric or profile condition, and exit
.B 4
if it does not hold. Repeatable. Accepts
.IR usable ,
.IR success ,
.IR median ,
.IR p95 ,
and
.I score
with
.BR >= ", " > ", " <= ", " < ", or " = ,
or
.IR winner=PROFILE-ID .
Rates are between 0 and 1; latencies accept a bare number of milliseconds or a
Go duration such as
.IR 50ms .
An expression may be prefixed with
.IR SUBJECT: ,
naming a resolver by profile id, by the name given to
.BR --resolver ,
or by target id. The check then applies to every measured endpoint of that
resolver rather than to the winners, which is how a gate expresses "did our
resolver degrade". An unmatched subject is invalid input. A subject endpoint
with no scored latency samples fails every
.IR median ,
.IR p95 ,
and
.I score
check whatever the operator, because there is no latency to compare;
.I usable
and
.I success
are still compared as rates.
.PP
Unqualified numeric checks apply to the rank-one target of each ranked protocol;
.I winner=
accepts any confidence-interval tie-group member.
A protocol whose endpoints all returned no usable DNS response also fails,
so a transport that is completely unreachable cannot pass the gate silently.
A transport that no selected resolver supports is not required, and a resolver
on the local host does not make its protocol required.
.TP
.B --include-system
Include resolvers discovered from the operating system configuration.
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable ANSI styling in table output.
.TP
.B --dnssec
Opt in to DNSSEC probing. Every query of the run carries the EDNS(0) DO bit
and each prepared target answers two additional pinned probe queries after its
measured rounds: a correctly signed control name that must resolve and a
deliberately mis-signed name that a validating resolver must refuse with
SERVFAIL. Up to --concurrency targets are probed at once, each with its two
probes in sequence. The report gains a per-target verdict of validating,
not-validating, or inconclusive, together with the raw AD and CD flags. The
probe describes those two names at that moment and is not a DNSSEC audit.
Without this option SpeeDNS sends its unchanged default queries and contacts
no probe names.
.SH OUTPUT
Table output is written to standard output by default. JSON output has a
versioned schema and CSV output has one aggregate row per target. Table runs
write progress only to standard error: terminals receive one updating line,
while redirected standard error receives deterministic preparation,
measurement, and completion lines. JSON and CSV runs are silent on standard
error.
.PP
Preparation covers connection setup, cold probes, and warm-up queries, and
runs with the same bounded concurrency as measurement. Every target finishes
its cold probes before any warm session opens, so until the first target is
fully prepared the line also counts targets whose cold probes have finished,
as in
.BR "preparing 0/10 (cold probes 7/10)" .
Measurement counts completed DNS exchanges, including failures. A --dnssec run
adds a
.B dnssec probes
phase after measurement that counts targets whose two probes have finished.
Progress does not show an ETA or resolver addresses and does not change report
contents.
.PP
Redirected progress reports each phase when it starts, again after roughly
every 25 percent of the work in that phase, and once more with its final
counters before the next phase or the completion line; cold probes and warm
sessions each count as half of preparation. That bounds the log at five lines
per phase and protocol. A phase whose total is small or zero still reports its
final counters.
.PP
Transport-library diagnostics use the same standard-error channel. Interactive
progress clears and restores its status line around such diagnostics. If all
selected IPv6 endpoints fail before receiving a usable response, table output
summarizes them as an unavailable IPv6 path and the default protocol
comparisons replace those endpoint rows with a hidden-endpoint count; partial
IPv6 failures remain visible per endpoint.
.PP
Paired latency effects compare every measured target against the best-ranked
target of the same protocol. A pair is formed only where both resolvers
returned the same response class, so a filtering resolver answering from its
blocklist is never compared against a real recursion. Resolvers on the local
host are excluded, since they are never ranked. The default table omits a
target that is the only measured member of its protocol, because such a row
compares a target with itself, and counts those below the block instead. The
--details view restores every hidden row, and JSON output is unchanged in both
views.
.PP
A qualified recommendation requires at least 20 scored comparable samples and
99 percent usable responses. A short run may show a provisional winner instead.
.SS Status vocabulary
.B QUALIFIED
meets the recommendation bar;
.B INELIGIBLE
does not, and the summary calls that same target
.B PROVISIONAL
when it is still the best-ranked one for its protocol.
.B NO ENDPOINT
means the resolver does not offer that transport.
.B NOT COMPARABLE
is measured but never ranked.
.B FAILED
and
.B INCOMPLETE
produced no usable result. The report prints this legend whenever a status
other than
.B QUALIFIED
appears.
.PP
Every table shows
.B Score 95% CI
beside the score, and
.B Min ,
the fastest observed exchange.
.SH DIFF
.B speedns diff BASELINE.json CURRENT.json
compares two saved JSON reports and reports what each resolver did
differently: reachability, response codes, whether it answered with a record,
and its DNSSEC verdict.
.PP
It never compares latency \(em not median, p95, score, rank, or any interval.
The difference between two runs is dominated by the network path and the time
of day, which no field in a report records, so no threshold computed from two
reports can bound it. To compare resolver speed, measure the resolvers together
in one run.
.PP
Both runs must have asked the same questions. The seed, sample size, query
types, corpus, timeout, concurrency, address family, DNSSEC setting and feature
version must match, and a
.B --cache-miss
run is never comparable with anything. When they do not match the diff is
declined, no count from either report is shown, and the exit status is 3.
.PP
Either report may be a pipe, /dev/stdin or a process substitution rather than
a regular file. Each is read up to 256 MiB, far more than a default report,
so an endless stream such as /dev/zero fails with status 2 and a size error.
.TP
.B --format table|json
Choose human-readable or machine-readable output. Defaults to table. There is
no CSV form: a comparison is not a per-target row set.
.TP
.B --require CONDITION
Exit 4 unless a named condition holds. Repeatable. Accepts
.IR no-new-failed-targets ,
.IR no-removed-targets ,
.IR target-set-identical ,
.IR no-dnssec-regression ,
and
.IR no-behaviour-change .
Every condition that does not hold is named on standard error, in the order
given. There is no threshold form, because the quantity one would read moves by
more than any useful threshold between identical runs. Use
.B --assert
on a run for an absolute bar.
.IP
.I no-new-failed-targets
fails when a target that answered in the baseline answers nothing in the
current run or newly cannot open a session, and when a target measured only in
the current run failed every query and is neither local nor interrupted. A
recovery passes.
.IP
When no endpoint was compared, because every target was interrupted, local,
redacted, or present in only one run, and no condition failed, the exit status
is 3 and the message counts the targets each reason excluded: a gate that read
nothing does not pass. A refusal outranks every condition.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The comparison completed successfully.
.TP
.B 2
Input or configuration was invalid.
.TP
.B 3
The run finished but produced no comparable DNS results. For
.BR "speedns diff" ,
the two runs were not comparable, or a
.B --require
gate compared no endpoint.
.TP
.B 4
A requested benchmark assertion or
.B diff --require
condition did not hold. The ordinary report or diff is written before this
status is returned.
.TP
.B 130
The run was interrupted. SpeeDNS reports
.B interrupted
on standard error and writes the report it has, with a warning that says
whether some target was left incomplete or missing, or whether the interrupt
arrived after measurement had completed. Assertions are not evaluated. A
second interrupt exits immediately.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/resolv.conf
System resolver configuration on Linux, and the fallback on macOS.
.TP
.I scutil --dns
Queried on macOS for scoped resolver configuration before the
.I /etc/resolv.conf
fallback.
.TP
.I GetAdaptersAddresses
Queried on Windows for per-adapter resolver configuration. Each adapter that
is up and configures a resolver becomes a separate target, labeled with the
adapter name Windows shows.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
speedns --protocol udp --sample 25 --type A --seed 42
speedns --format json --output result.json
speedns --protocol doq --sample 25 2>speedns-progress.log
speedns completion bash > speedns.bash
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR resolv.conf (5)
